## Current Conclusion

This controlled run compares the dense triplet, matched single-mouse, and
within-mouse triplet checkpoints. Each uses the same 800 source training
windows, 50 epochs, seed 42, temporal contract, and matched optimizer-update
budget. The diagnostic uses 30 held-out triplet windows expanded into 90 paired
mouse cases. Typical P50 results over the complete prediction horizon are:

| mode | model | cADE (px) | BLE (px) | SOE (deg) | BHE (deg) | ROE |
| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| teacher-forced mean | dense | 4.38 | 4.17 | 9.20 | 5.09 | 0.061 |
| teacher-forced mean | single-mouse | 3.64 | 4.11 | 8.96 | 5.00 | 0.062 |
| teacher-forced mean | within-mouse | 3.76 | 4.49 | 9.98 | 4.86 | 0.069 |
| autoregressive mean | dense | 42.85 | 14.90 | 30.71 | 24.06 | 0.192 |
| autoregressive mean | single-mouse | 29.61 | 12.57 | 28.42 | 20.97 | 0.196 |
| autoregressive mean | within-mouse | 28.93 | 13.10 | 29.54 | 21.51 | 0.180 |
| autoregressive sample | dense | 71.73 | 102.78 | 68.70 | 55.99 | 0.389 |
| autoregressive sample | single-mouse | 41.68 | 26.68 | 67.90 | 61.05 | 0.389 |
| autoregressive sample | within-mouse | 108.65 | 39.68 | 68.25 | 60.05 | 0.389 |

Against dense triplet, the matched single-mouse model improves sampled BLE by
87.85 px on average (95% cluster CI -101.02 to -74.27) and improves it in every
paired case. It also improves body-frame keypoint ADE by 35.16 px (-45.53 to
-25.02) and FDE by 68.62 px (-97.72 to -40.78). Its cADE difference is -3.74 px
(-16.28 to 8.32), so sampled trajectory accuracy is not reliably different.
The SOE, BHE, and ROE intervals also contain zero.

The within-mouse triplet retains its previously established effects: BLE
improves by 77.44 px (-90.79 to -63.82), while cADE worsens by 30.79 px (15.17
to 46.53), CVE by 24.54 px/s (18.30 to 31.13), and KVE by 57.74 px/s (48.31 to
67.68). Whole source windows are resampled so the three mice are not treated as
independent observations.

Neither no-cross-mouse-edge variant reliably improves SOE, BHE, or ROE. The
supported shared effect is improved body scale. Only the single-mouse model also
shows a reliable improvement in body-frame keypoint displacement.

The deterministic Gaussian-mean rollout is better for within-mouse than dense
on cADE (28.93 versus 42.85 px), while its sampled cADE is worse. This reversal
comes from uncertainty rather than mean dynamics. At the final sampled step,
within-mouse sigma is about 36 px on x and 28 px on y, compared with 15 px and
13 px for dense. Single-mouse lies between them at about 18 px on both axes and
has the best final 95% coverage, 12.9%, versus 5.2% for dense and 4.4% for
within-mouse. All remain severely under-covered during autoregressive rollout.

In this controlled run, isolating a single mouse preserves scale and mouse-local
keypoint positions without a statistically reliable trajectory penalty. Merely
removing cross-mouse edges while retaining all three mice preserves scale but
worsens sampled trajectory stability. This suggests that the single-mouse gain
is not explained by cross-edge removal alone; reducing the graph to one actor is
also material. Because every model has one trained seed, these are within-run
findings rather than general causal claims. The bootstrap measures held-out-
window uncertainty, not training-run variation.

## Plot Per-Horizon Errors

Each point measures the error at that exact future frame. It is not an average
from prediction step 1 through the displayed step. Solid lines are
autoregressive rollouts; dashed lines are teacher-forced predictions. A widening
gap between them identifies accumulated rollout error.
Each model uses the same seeded Gaussian draws in both modes so the comparison
does not change merely because a different random stream was sampled.

The plots cover trajectory displacement, transition velocity, pose scale,
orientation, heading, relative ordering, and body-frame keypoint error. P50
shows the typical paired case, while P99 exposes tail failures.

```python {"interpreter":"/home/splion/Desktop/personal/interpreting-social-navigation/.venv/bin/python"}
import json
import os
from pathlib import Path
import sys

from IPython.display import Image, display

repo_root = next(path for path in [Path.cwd(), *Path.cwd().parents] if (path / "src").exists())
os.environ.setdefault("MPLCONFIGDIR", str(repo_root / "outputs/matplotlib"))
sys.path.insert(0, str(repo_root / "src"))

from diagnostics import save_horizon_profile_figure

result_dir = repo_root / "outputs/evaluations/pose_diagnostics_5fps"
record = json.loads((result_dir / "results.jsonl").read_text().splitlines()[-1])

for statistic in ("p50", "p99"):
    figure_path = save_horizon_profile_figure(
        record,
        result_dir / f"horizon_profile_{statistic}.png",
        statistic=statistic,
    )
    display(Image(filename=str(figure_path)))
```

## Inspect Worst Relative-Ordering Cases

```python {"interpreter":"/home/splion/Desktop/personal/interpreting-social-navigation/.venv/bin/python"}
import json
import os
from pathlib import Path
import sys

repo_root = next(path for path in [Path.cwd(), *Path.cwd().parents] if (path / "src").exists())
os.environ.setdefault("MPLCONFIGDIR", str(repo_root / "outputs/matplotlib"))
sys.path.insert(0, str(repo_root / "src"))

case_path = repo_root / "outputs/evaluations/pose_diagnostics_5fps/cases.jsonl"
rows = [json.loads(line) for line in case_path.read_text().splitlines()]
single_rows = [
    row for row in rows
    if row["model"] == "single_mouse" and row["mode"] == "autoregressive"
]
single_rows = sorted(
    single_rows,
    key=lambda row: row["metrics"]["relative_ordering_error"],
    reverse=True,
)

for row in single_rows[:10]:
    print(
        row["sequence_id"],
        "start=", row["start_frame"],
        "mouse=", row["mouse_index"],
        "seed=", row["seed"],
        "motion=", row["motion_group"],
        "ROE=", round(row["metrics"]["relative_ordering_error"], 4),
        "BLE=", round(row["metrics"]["bone_length_error_px"], 3),
        "SOE=", round(row["metrics"]["skeleton_orientation_error_deg"], 3),
    )
```
